itute of Science and Technology","Pembroke College"],"occupations":["politician","chemical engineer","university teacher","engineer"],"wiki":"George Maxwell Richards"},"Q57712":{"id":"Q57712","name":"Kamla Persad-Bissessar","desc":"politician from Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/2025_Kamla_Persad-Bissessar_(cropped).jpg/330px-2025_Kamla_Persad-Bissessar_(cropped).jpg","gender":"F","born":"1952-04-22","died":null,"birthplace":"Siparia","parties":["United National Congress"],"education":["University of the West Indies","Hugh Wooding Law School"],"occupations":["politician","lawyer"],"wiki":"Kamla Persad-Bissessar"},"Q4772220":{"id":"Q4772220","name":"Anthony Carmona","desc":"President of Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Anthony_Carmona.jpg/330px-Anthony_Carmona.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1953-03-07","died":null,"birthplace":"Fyzabad","parties":[],"education":["University of the West Indies","Presentation College, San Fernando","Hugh Wooding Law School"],"occupations":["judge","politician","lawyer"],"wiki":"Anthony Carmona"},"Q6384971":{"id":"Q6384971","name":"Keith Rowley","desc":"politician from Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Trinidad_and_Tobago_Prime_Minister_Keith_Rowley_on_6_July_2023_-_(53027345558)_(cropped).jpg/330px-Trinidad_and_Tobago_Prime_Minister_Keith_Rowley_on_6_July_2023_-_(53027345558)_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1949-10-24","died":null,"birthplace":"Tobago","parties":["People's National Movement"],"education":["University of the West Indies"],"occupations":["politician","geologist"],"wiki":"Keith Rowley"},"Q47461098":{"id":"Q47461098","name":"Paula-Mae Weekes","desc":"President of Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Paula-Mae_Weekes_2019.jpg/330px-Paula-Mae_Weekes_2019.jpg","gender":"F","born":"1958-12-23","died":null,"birthplace":"Port of Spain","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["Hugh Wooding Law School","University of the West Indies at Cave Hill"],"occupations":["jurist","politician","lawyer"],"wiki":"Paula-Mae Weekes"},"Q97400315":{"id":"Q97400315","name":"Christine Kangaloo","desc":"politician from Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/The_Hon_Christine_Kangaloo.jpg/330px-The_Hon_Christine_Kangaloo.jpg","gender":"F","born":"1961-12-01","died":null,"birthplace":"San Fernando","parties":["People's National Movement"],"education":["University of the West Indies"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Christine Kangaloo"},"Q107504646":{"id":"Q107504646","name":"Stuart Young","desc":"politician in Trinidad and Tobago","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Stuart_Young_2016_(cropped).jpg/330px-Stuart_Young_2016_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1975-02-09","died":null,"birthplace":"Port of Spain","parties":["People's National Movement"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician","lawyer"],"wiki":"Stuart Young"}},"summary":"Trinidad and Tobago, officially the Republic of Trinidad and Tobago, is the southernmost archipelagic country in the Caribbean, comprising the main islands of Trinidad and Tobago, along with several smaller islets. The capital city is Port of Spain, while its largest and most populous municipality is Chaguanas. Trinidad and Tobago comprises the southernmost islands of the Caribbean eastern islands chain, and it is close to the continent of South America, being north to northeast of Venezuela and northwest of Guyana.   \nTrinidad and Tobago is located six nautical miles (11 km; 6.9 mi) north to northeast off the coast of Venezuela, 70 nautical miles (130 km; 81 mi) south of Grenada, and 155 nautical miles (287 km; 178 mi) southwest of Barbados. Indigenous peoples inhabited Trinidad for centuries prior to Spanish colonisation, following the arrival of Christopher Columbus in 1498. Spanish governor José María Chacón surrendered the island to a British fleet under Ralph Abercromby's command in 1797. Trinidad and Tobago were ceded to Britain in 1802 under the Treaty of Amiens as separate states and unified in 1889. Trinidad and Tobago obtained independence in 1962, and became a republic in 1976.\nUnlike most Caribbean countries and territories, which rely heavily on tourism, the economy is primarily industrial, based on large reserves of oil and gas. The country sees fewer hurricanes than most of the Caribbean because it is farther south.\nTrinidad and Tobago is well known for its African and Indian  cultures, reflected in its large and famous Carnival, Diwali, and Hosay celebrations, as well as being the birthplace of the steelpan, the limbo, and musical styles such as calypso, soca, rapso, chutney, and chutney soca.","history":"The history of Trinidad and Tobago begins with the settlements of the islands by Indigenous First Peoples. Trinidad was visited by Christopher Columbus on his third voyage in 1498, (he never landed in Tobago), and claimed in the name of Spain. Trinidad was administered by Spanish hands until 1797, but it was largely settled by French colonists. Tobago changed hands between the British, French, Dutch, and Courlanders, but eventually ended up in British hands following the second Treaty of Paris (1814). In 1889, the two islands were incorporated into a single political entity. Trinidad and Tobago obtained its independence from the British Empire in 1962 and became a republic in 1976.","historyTitle":"History of Trinidad and Tobago","curated":null,"indicators":{"gdpPerCapita":[[1960,633.976],[1961,677.714],[1962,703.621],[1963,756.285],[1964,779.766],[1965,793.358],[1966,767.538],[1967,796.704],[1968,783.174],[1969,794.53],[1970,828.92],[1971,894.453],[1972,1067.46],[1973,1273.32],[1974,1962.03],[1975,2318.69],[1976,2344.93],[1977,2907.37],[1978,3258.09],[1979,4155.16],[1980,5559.05],[1981,6159.71],[1982,7091.41],[1983,6689.81],[198